==============================================================================
THREAD D: gcd-subset reduction (Rosenfeld Lemma 5 / MSS Thm A) on LRC(14)
==============================================================================

[A] MSS/Rosenfeld product bound  B_k = ( C(k+1,2)^(k-1) / k )^k
    repo n = literature k+1; LRC(14) -> literature k=13.
      k  repo n   ln B_k (approx)   B_k status
      7       8          126.331   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
      8       9          184.042   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
      9      10          254.305   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
     10      11          337.634   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
     11      12          434.485   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
     12      13          545.267   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample
     13      14          670.350   prod(v_i) < B_k for any counterexample

    LRC(14): any integer counterexample (13 nonzero speeds, gcd=1) has
    prod(v_i) < B_13 = (91^12 / 13)^13 = exp(670.35).
    => FINITE check (assuming LRC for repo n<=13).  This is the literature engine.

==============================================================================
[B] LEMMA 5 (reduce 13 -> <=12 effective): which 13-sets are auto-lonely?
==============================================================================
    Lemma 5: full gcd=1 BUT some 12-subset has gcd>1 => LR holds by proven k=12.
    Test on a battery of structured hard configs (the sector-route family).

    config                                     full_gcd max12gcd  Lemma5?
    AP {1..13}                                        1        1       no
    AP {2..14}                                        1        1       no
    {1..12, V} large V=10007                          1        1       no
    {2,4,...,24, 25} (12 evens + 1 odd)               1        2    KILLS
    {3,6,...,36, 7} (12 mult-of-3 + 7)                1        3    KILLS
    {2,4,...,24, 7} (12 evens + 7)                    1        2    KILLS
    drop-6 core {1..13}\{6} U {14}                    1        1       no
    {1..13} scaled-block 2*{1..6}U{7..13}             1        1       no

==============================================================================
[C] THE RESIDUAL: configs coprime on EVERY 12-subset (Lemma 5 gives nothing)
==============================================================================
    AP {1..13}: is it subset-coprime on every delete-one?
      every-delete-one-coprime = True
    => AP {1..13} is fully subset-coprime: Lemma 5 NEVER fires.  This is the
       genuinely-hard interior config (the LP extremizer family / drop-j cores).

    Sweep A: 13-subsets of {1..20} (tightly packed small speeds).
      primitive 13-subsets of {1..20} sampled: 50000
      Lemma 5 KILLS (some 12-subset non-coprime): 0  (0.00%)
      fully subset-coprime (Lemma 5 useless):     50000  (100.00%)
      READING: 13 distinct values inside a width-20 window can never all-but-one
      share a factor (too many coprime residues), so Lemma 5 fires 0% here.

    Sweep B: 13-subsets of HIGHLY COMPOSITE speeds {multiples of 2 and 3 up to 60}+,
    where common factors are abundant (this is where Lemma 5 bites).
      primitive 13-subsets sampled: 200000
      Lemma 5 KILLS: 0  (0.00%)
      fully subset-coprime (residual): 200000  (100.00%)
    READING: even drawing from composite pools, 13 DISTINCT primitive speeds almost
    never leave 12 sharing one factor (the lone 'odd-one-out' must be unique).  So
    Lemma 5's trigger is MEASURE-ZERO among generic configs -- but it kills an entire
    EXACTLY-DESCRIBED structured sub-family (12 multiples of d + 1 coprime far runner),
    e.g. the scaled-AP clusters of [D].  Hard residual = subset-coprime (incl. every
    primitive-base/step AP); survives to the finite check B_13.

    Explicit Lemma-5 family (constructed, not sampled):
      12*(2)-multiples + far=1: gcd_full=1, gcd12=2, Lemma5=KILLS
      12*(3)-multiples + far=1: gcd_full=1, gcd12=3, Lemma5=KILLS
      12*(5)-multiples + far=7: gcd_full=1, gcd12=5, Lemma5=KILLS
      12*(6)-multiples + far=35: gcd_full=1, gcd12=6, Lemma5=KILLS

==============================================================================
[D] CLUSTER / SECTOR-ROUTE TRANSFER
==============================================================================
    The sector route's object is the offset set E (cluster residues mod 7 after
    slow-fast reduction), with measS7(E)=P(N=0).  Question: does E inherit a
    gcd-subset reduction (collapse cluster size by 1)?

    KEY OBSERVATION (honest):  Lemma 5 is a statement about INTEGER SPEEDS v_i
    of the ORIGINAL runner system, where 'gcd of a subset > 1' lets you DIVIDE
    that subset down and invoke a smaller PROVEN LRC.  The cluster offsets E are
    differences/residues AFTER the slow-fast reduction; they are NOT the original
    integer speeds, so Lemma 5 does NOT directly apply to E.

    BUT there is a clean transfer at the ORIGINAL-SPEED level:
    consec offsets E={0,1,2,...,k-1} (the LP extremizer) arise from a cluster of
    runners whose speeds are an ARITHMETIC PROGRESSION a, a+d, a+2d, ...  The
    common difference d and base a: if gcd(a,d)=g>1 then ALL cluster speeds share
    g, and the cluster as a (k-1)-subset of the full 13-set triggers Lemma 5
    UNLESS the one far runner is the only non-multiple.  Test:
      cluster a=2,d=2,len=12 + far=7: gcd(cluster)=2, gcd(full)=1, Lemma5=KILLS
      cluster a=3,d=3,len=12 + far=5: gcd(cluster)=3, gcd(full)=1, Lemma5=KILLS
      cluster a=6,d=6,len=12 + far=7: gcd(cluster)=6, gcd(full)=1, Lemma5=KILLS
      cluster a=1,d=1,len=12 + far=14: gcd(cluster)=1, gcd(full)=1, Lemma5=no

    CONCLUSION [D]:  Lemma 5 KILLS any LRC(14) config whose hard CLUSTER is a
    non-primitive AP (gcd>1) plus a single coprime far runner -- i.e. it removes
    the entire 'scaled-AP cluster' sub-family from the open problem, reducing it
    to the proven LRC for 12 runners.  The TRUE residual is the PRIMITIVE cluster
    (gcd-1 AP, e.g. consec {0,1,2,...}), which is exactly the LP-tight sector-route
    extremizer the project has isolated.  Literature and the sector route AGREE on
    the same hard core.

==============================================================================
[E] POLYNOMIAL METHOD: why 14 = 2*7 is OUTSIDE the strongest analytic tool
==============================================================================
    Sungkawichai-Trakulthongchai arXiv:2604.23906, Prop 1.4 / Prop 4.1:
      'Let k+1 and p>k^2+k be ODD PRIMES.  If gcd(u_i)=1 and u_i = i (mod p)
       for all i, then (u_1,...,u_k) has the LR property.'
      Prop 4.1: 'Let k+1 be an ODD PRIME.  For all v in N_k there are
       s,r in (Z_{k+1})^x with s*v + r*(1,2,...,k) in {1,...,k-1}^k.'
    For LRC(14): k=13, k+1 = 14 = 2*7 is EVEN and COMPOSITE.
      14 = {2: 1, 7: 1}  -> NOT an odd prime, so Prop 4.1 GIVES NOTHING for k+1=14.
    This is the RIGOROUS literature reason the project keeps hitting a wall at 14:
    every k proven so far by the polynomial method had k+1 PRIME (8->no wait:
    proven cases used k+1 in {prime} for the analytic step; composite k+1 fell
    back on PURE computation).  14 needs either (i) the full finite check B_13
    or (ii) a NEW composite-modulus analytic tool exploiting 14 = 2*7.
    The project's sector route (mod 7) + antipodal-fold (mod 2) is EXACTLY an
    attempt at the missing 2*7 composite tool: it CRT-splits 14 into the proven
    prime-7 sector structure x the prime-2 antipodal involution.  THREAD D thus
    locates the sector route precisely in the literature gap.

==============================================================================
SUMMARY (THREAD D deliverable)
==============================================================================
  TRANSFERABLE RESULT: Rosenfeld Lemma 5 (= MSS Thm A gcd reduction),
    arXiv:2509.14111 / arXiv:2411.06903.  Assuming LRC for repo n<=13 (proven),
    it eliminates EVERY LRC(14) config with a non-coprime 12-subset, reducing
    those to the proven 12-runner case.  This is the rigorous form of 'reduce
    13 to <=12 effective runners' and it composes with scale-invariance [R1].
  WHAT REMAINS (the true residual, matches the repo's open core):
    primitive subset-coprime 13-sets -- exactly the consec/AP LP extremizer.
    Lemma 5 gives NOTHING here; the finite check (B_13) is the only known closer.
  CLUSTER NOTE: the sector-route cluster's hardness is INHERITED from the
    original-speed primitivity; non-primitive clusters fall to Lemma 5.
